{
  "description": "Decision-layer fixtures for decideBash: SC20's named-path rule (a write-class piece naming a primary-checkout path is denied from any cwd, while a read-class piece naming the same path and the outside-any-repo uncertain class stay allowed), the destination scope of that rule (a write command's read sources, verb words, flags, and non-path flag values -- a git commit message, a cp source -- are not write targets and are left unjudged, so SC16's `git commit -m \"$(date)\"` is allowed from a worktree and denied from a primary checkout), SC15's digest-verified provisioned-CLI allowance (four verbs, no retarget, no cleanup-forcing --force, keyed on binary identity supplied as argv), and the membership walk's indifference to whether a write target already exists (a target that is itself a file or a device node makes its own `.git` probe an ENOTDIR, which answers the membership question rather than clouding it, so an existing-file write earns the same verdict as a new-path one -- while a genuine lstat failure anywhere on the climb still denies). The loader builds one fixed hermetic topology per case: primary checkout /repo, linked worktree /repo/wt, an already-existing tracked.md in each, a device node at /dev/null, provisioned CLI at /plugin-data/bin/git-tools. arg_path/arg_digest default to the correct provisioned path and the sha256 of the binary's bytes; 'omit' passes an empty argv parameter, 'wrong' passes a valid-but-mismatched digest, and a literal overrides. bin_present=false withholds the binary so it cannot be re-hashed. err_at makes one path's lstat fail indeterminately. The sc15 redirect pairs pin what voids that allowance: a bare fd duplication (2>&1, 2>&-) opens no file and leaves a landing call allowed, identical to its no-redirect baseline, while any redirect that opens something -- a file-writing one, a lookalike target that is really a path, an input redirect, a here-document -- voids it, including when a benign fd-dup sits alongside on the same command. The `>1` case now pins the reversal of that deferral (A21): a bare-digit or `-` target duplicates a descriptor only when the matched operator itself carries the `&` (`>&`, `<&`, and their numeric-fd-prefixed forms), so under the plain `>` here the digit is the literal file bash opens, the sc15 allowance is voided, and the write lands in the primary checkout. A parallel exemption (A1) keeps a `/dev/null` discard from ever reading as a write, under any operator, including a bare redirected `git status`. A6 adds the CLI's second, read-only allowance: the digest-verified provisioned CLI's `worktree list` reclassifies from the fail-closed uncertain default to read, bare or carrying a `--repo`/`--config` flag (a read names no repo it could be retargeted onto, so those flags don't void it the way they void a write). The allow-side pair pins that. Every other CLI verb -- push, sign, resign, rebase, branch create/delete, hooks install, and worktree add/remove, including both `--dry-run` spellings -- still denies when its own write path is unavailable (voided by retarget for the four landing verbs, never sanctioned at all for the rest), proving the read reclassification is keyed on the exact two-token verb and lets nothing else through, worktree add and worktree remove least of all since they share `worktree list`'s own leading token. The retarget-voided merge pair now carries all four spellings, `--repo`/`--config` spaced and glued. Worktree remove is the fourth sanctioned landing verb, allowed from a primary checkout exactly as worktree add is -- its bare, `--dry-run`, and `--landing-target` spellings allowed, its retarget spelling denied -- since it runs its own no-work-loss guard inside the CLI. A `--force` on either cleanup path -- merge or worktree remove -- voids the sanction the way a retargeting flag does, because a forced cleanup drives the CLI past that guard on a tree the gate cannot see; the same call without `--force` stays allowed, as does merge's opt-in `--cleanup` (which triggers the guarded cleanup, not a forced one). This is the gate's refusal meaning of --force, distinct from the CLI's own --force, which only overrides a refusal where the gate already permits the call. worktree add's --force is a different concern and stays sanctioned. Every sc15 identity negative (digest mismatch, missing/omitted argv parameter, unreadable binary, bare/relative/arbitrary-path name) and every redirect-void negative (stdout/stderr to a file, an input redirect, a heredoc, both fd-dup-then-file orderings) gets a `worktree list` counterpart, because the read allowance shares sc15Identity with the write one and so carries the same identity requirement and the same void. FB7 adds the worktree-home exemption: a named target under <primary>/.claude/worktrees/ that is not itself a live worktree is gate-managed scratch, allowed from the two cwd positions the over-block reproduced in (outside any repo, inside a worktree) but still denied from a primary-checkout cwd, since rm stays a write prefix and that residual is disclosed rather than fixed here; a non-scratch target elsewhere under the same primary checkout stays denied regardless of cwd.",
